\cf2 Have you noticed, there is a pattern, where Ezekiel 20:45-49 has several elements of a riddle or parable, and Ezekiel 21:1-7 has the corresponding elements of the interpretation? Ezekiel objects when he is given this riddle, in that people speak dismissing him as a maker of riddles. But this objection is answered thrice over; the riddle is given an interpretation which elevates Ezekiel above a simple riddler, the objection corresponds in the interpretation to an embodied sign of groaning, and the groan will in turn lead to a question which drives the rhetoric of this oracle far beyond the riddle starting point.\
In that they may be more familiar, I am using the English verse numbers - but note that the Hebrew verse numbers start with verse 1 of chapter 21 where the English has 20:45.\
In the complexity of its structure, the force of its emotions, and the extremes of its theological language, this oracle challenges us to look carefully at the evidence to understand Ezekiel's rhetoric, purpose, and methods.
