\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I would add additional correspondences:\
The paired announcements that the word of the LORD came to him The paired addresses as son of man The paired commands to speak The paired thus has the LORD declared The paired behold Consuming every tree / flame not extinguished -> cut off righteous and wicked (in similarity with the forest fire -> sword correspondence you mentioned) every face scorched -> sword drawn against all flesh (same pattern) I have kindled it it will not be extinguished -> I have drawn my sword it will not return.\
Yes, chapter 18 was quite clear, as you say - and chapters 9 and 14 imply as much as well. The fate of the individual sinner being linked to his own guilt is a sufficiently repeated theme in Ezekiel that we have at least some reason to suspect that the theological claim that the sword will cut off both righteous and wicked does not entirely contradict the message of the earlier chapters. But in what way it does not contradict will need to be determined by the evidence.\
Before we plunge into that evidence though, I would like to add some additional hypotheses you did not mention:\
The sword as a standard metaphor for war in all its tendencies to destroy without regard.\
Righteous and Wicked together as a merism for everyone, in keeping with the other uses of all and every in the oracle.\
The righteous exiled with the purpose of destroying the wicked.\
Deliberate subversion of more standard theological language to attack misunderstandings of that language.\
Some, but not all, of these hypotheses are consistent enough with each other that they might together become part of a more nuanced understanding of the purpose of this theological statement.\
And yes, I was being terse mentioning the three-fold answer. Anything unclear, by all means bring up. To start, you see the tight correspondences between 20:45 to 20:48 and 21:1 to 21:6, no? Immediately following 20:48 and 21:6, the oracle turns to Ezekiel in both cases. The first, Ezekiel talking to the LORD about what the people say. The second, the LORD talking to Ezekiel about what Ezekiel is to communicate before the eyes of the people. This seems to suggest that the correspondence continues. The discontent of the people with Ezekiel, and Ezekiel's discontent with that position is then, part of what is to be interpreted by that which corresponds to it, the embodied sign of groaning, as of a man with crushed testicles. Extreme, but in keeping with the rest of the oracle. The people are discontent now with the one warning them - but in the future, they will be beyond discontent with what is warned of. This mirrors the riddle, which warns, and the interpretation, which says what is warned of. Ezekiel's understandable reaction to the discontent against him corresponds to groaning, as a sign.\
But if this remains unconvincing, by all means say so.
