\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I will try to answer your remaining uncertainty then.\
Ezekiel is, of course, a riddler. He is instructed to use a riddle several times. But, as in earlier chapters, he is not doing this to be confusing or entertaining. The riddle is given an interpretation, which acts as a warning which may get around some defenses that the more straightforward warnings elsewhere in Ezekiel might deflect.\
And yes, starting with the Ah, Ezekiel seems quite aware that the people are dismissing him as a prophet, presumably due to their rebellion against the One who sent him as a prophet, and their stony hearts - subjects of several earlier chapters. So there is every possibility that even with the riddle, the interpretation, the sign, and the question the sign provokes, the people will not believe.\
The progression beyond riddle to interpretation, sign, question, and answer may elevate Ezekiel beyond mere riddler. But more so, what will elevate him is the things warned of coming to pass - see verse 7, behold it is coming, and it will be fulfilled. Even more so, it is his standing as one to whom the word of the LORD comes, who utters the declaration of the LORD, which elevates him above a riddler. But even so, Ezekiel seems frustrated with the people, in their rebellion, their failure to listen, and their discontent. Ultimately, the only answer he gets to that is the groaning that will come to those same people when news of disaster reaches them.\
However, having said this, your uncertainty is very appropriate. The evidence for this position which I have presented, even now, is by no means overwhelming. I offer it as one thing Ezekiel's objection might be doing in this text, because one does not properly understand the text until one can explain all its elements.\
Turning to the question of the righteous and the wicked, I would say that the absence of discussion about what happens to the righteous, independent of the wicked, is clearly intentional. That is, the message is not about the righteous, as such.\
As as of yet disconnected pieces of evidence, I offer the following:\
Fire is indiscriminate like the sword, in its all devouring nature.\
Cutting off is language evocative of Leviticus casuistry, as Ezekiel 18 was. Consider, for example, Leviticus 20, which puts cutting off in parallel with the LORD setting his face against, which is also found in Ezekiel 21:2.\
While the judgement on Jerusalem is very much in view, perhaps even more so is the reaction to that news by the audience of exiles.\
All flesh is used of those who see, of those who know the LORD as the LORD, and of those the sword is drawn against. In that last use, it is supplemented with the further all encompassing phrase 'from south to north'. The uses of these phrases may be further connected by the end of verse 5, 'it shall not be sheathed again', in that the sovereignty of the LORD in drawing the sword, the purpose in self revelation, and the not relenting until the all encompassing nature of the judgement accomplishes that purpose, have a clear logical connection.\
We ought to take very seriously the purpose clause with which verse 4 starts. This is perhaps our best clue.
