The exiles are the audience for this oracle, and both by audience and by the nature of the structural shift to the exiles, we have reason to think that a large part of the rhetorical purpose is aimed at them, in all their rebellion, false hopes, and theological misconceptions. So let us examine the larger rhetorical structure. \
The exiles are given a riddle which is quite difficult to interpret themselves. Then, they are given an interpretation which mirrors the riddle. But that interpretation goes against their rebellion - their hopes are dashed, their theology overturned forcefully, their attempts at life independent from the LORD run into the LORD's sovereignty, and they are part of this all flesh that will see and know. To a great degree, that is a hypothesis from earlier chapters, but I think it confirmed by the evidence of this oracle. \
Then, Ezekiel embodies the sign of groaning. Here, we must make a distinction between the rhetoric of the text, and the rhetoric of the original prophetic sign. In the text, the structure where the sign mirrors Ezekiel's complaint is highly relevant. But the oral rather than written original audience would presumably not know that. But they do have the sign, their own question, and the answer to it.\
