\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Earlier chapters of Ezekiel do seem to have a repeated pattern of attacking false hopes, not just those of righteous remnants.\
Remnant, though not specifically righteous, was a theme of Ezekiel 5. That false hope was thrown into a fire, that is, the hairs representing it were. Jerusalem's role, visible in the midst of nations, was foregrounded there - as was the abomination they put on display, shaking a different false hope in the presence of the LORD's temple. In Ezekiel 5, the knowledge of the LORD comes from knowledge that the LORD has spoken in jealousy, when they see the fury, from the nations seeing the horror.\
Remnant, specifically not righteous, was a theme of Ezekiel 6. There, the knowledge of the LORD comes from the remnant recognizing their sin, how their sin was objectively bad, deserving of punishment, grieved the LORD, and drew predicted punishment.\
Remnant, specifically not righteous, was a theme of Ezekiel 7. But the false hopes dashed there are money, false Gods, and the LORD's apparent inaction. \
We do see a righteous remnant in Ezekiel 9, with the bare minimum standard of grieving over the abominations the people commit. But thorough judgment falls, even on women and children, in the very next verse. And Ezekiel is prompted to ask whether this means the LORD will destroy all the remnant. But this is not addressing a false hope in that remnant - explicitly, it addresses the false belief 'The LORD has forsaken the land, and the LORD does not see.'\
Ezekiel 10 addresses false hopes in temple, but not in a righteous remnant. Though it does emphasize the holiness and sovereignty of the LORD.\
Ezekiel 11 addresses false hopes inside the city of Jerusalem, though these false hopes are in human greatness of some sort perhaps, not in a righteous remnant. To the degree remnant is in view, it is in the unrighteous fleeing and being cut down, and in Ezekiel asking whether a full end would be made of the remnant. Any hope in the righteous remnant is in those already exiled, and that, in the LORD's action to make them righteous.\
Ezekiel 12 addresses false hopes of delay and inaction on the part of the LORD. Both Ezekiel 12 and 13 also attack false hopes placed in false visions.\
We already discussed Ezekiel 14.\
It is in Ezekiel 16 that we start to see a pattern of false hopes in righteousness. There, supposed past righteousness hopes are subverted and shockingly dashed. \
Ezekiel 17 dashes royal hopes.\
Ezekiel 18 dashes hopes in more recent past righteousness. A righteous father, or a recent righteous monarch, cannot rescue wicked sons. The wicked must repent - and indeed, the LORD wants them to, as he has no pleasure in the death of anyone. But they must turn to live.\
Ezekiel 19 dashes royal hopes, and subverts any hope in royal righteousness.\
But it is Ezekiel 20, the chapter immediately preceding 21, which most thoroughly dashes hopes in righteousness. It thoroughly subverts hopes in past righteousness, and then discusses how the remnant will need purged before it will be accepted. This will manifest the LORD's holiness among them in the sight of the nations. It is the LORD's care for that purged remnant which will bring knowledge of the LORD - along with remembering their past unrighteousness. Ultimately, the chapter is about the LORD's concern for his name uprooting all false understanding of the LORD's actions, past, present, and future.
