\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I think you meant English verse 13, not 23. By all means, check if you at all suspect I have made such an error. I am of course using appropriate caution, but mistakes are easy to make what with the Hebrew to English verse offset.\
Verse 13 (English) is grammatically difficult at best, that is, we have to make some grammatical and interpretational choices. Some of those choices are easier than others. For instance, grammatical gender is a guide here. Despises is feminine, as is sword, so the subject of despises is sword. Similarly, be is masculine, as is scepter, so the subject of scepter is masculine.\
Other choices are harder. For instance, it is entirely legal to insert it is, before testing. Skipping such words is entirely normal in Hebrew. But neither are we required to read it that way. Perhaps we should prefer the more coherent alternative.\
Similarly, we need to interpret the 'and' before 'what'.\
Consider the following reading: For a testing... then what?\
That is, there was a time of testing, but the question is, what follows when that testing is over? If we interpret testing as testing a sword, a standard part of high quality sword manufacture, what follows when that testing is over? Use of the sword. Against what? Against the royal house, despising whatever protections or legitimacy or claims others might think the royal house has, or perhaps the royal house itself, or even the trees as a whole, that is, the people, though perhaps in the context of the people giving strength to the monarchy. That reading makes sense of the unstated object of despising and an unfinished 'even if the scepter', reading both as allowing the reader to think as broadly as possible about those protections and the like, only to have them dismissed. The result? The scepter will not be. This interpretation then explains the links to earlier verses discussing the sword, its preparation by sharpening, and the scepter.\
This is, admittedly, not the clearest verse. The grammar is difficult. This is the most coherent option for how to take the grammar I can think of - but if you can think of a better interpretation, this verse is difficult enough that I would be quite prepared to consider it very seriously. I can at best give a provisional reading.\
Verse 10, English, is also quite difficult to read. Here too, we perhaps have false hope in God's promises to the royal line - thus scepter, my son, and so on. I make, here, a similar point about grammatical gender. Despises is feminine, as is sword and not scepter. Therefore, it is the sword which despises every tree. This fits with my reading of verse 13, English.\
